<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Fougères</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Fougères is a town in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ille-et-vilaine">Ille-et-Vilaine</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of north-eastern Brittany. Its castle, one of the largest medieval fortresses in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, guarded the Breton border with Normandy and Maine. The town was seized by a captain in English service in 1449, an act that restarted the war and led to the French reconquest of Normandy, and it was taken by the French in 1488 shortly before the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-saint-aubin-du-cormier">Battle of Saint-Aubin-du-Cormier</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fougeres.jpg" alt="Fougères" width="320" height="479" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Benjiwix · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Foug%C3%A8res.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Fougères is a commune and sub-prefecture of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ille-et-vilaine">Ille-et-Vilaine</a>, in Brittany, on the edge of Brittany, Maine and Normandy. It lies 40 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rennes">Rennes</a>, 58 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mont-saint-michel">Mont-Saint-Michel</a> and 330 kilometres from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>. Megalithic monuments in the forest of Fougères suggest that the area was inhabited in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a> era. The town stood at the crossing of two <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/roman-roads">Roman roads</a>, one from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chartres">Chartres</a> to Carhaix and the other from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/avranches">Avranches</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nantes">Nantes</a>. It is built on rock, and the castle stands not on a height but on a deposit of hard metamorphic rock, which protected it from attack by mining and from underground.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The creation of Fougères dates to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. Its castle was first mentioned around the end of the 10th century as a simple wooden fortification on a rocky ridge dominating the Nançon valley and its marshes. From the 12th century the population moved away from the banks of the Nançon, and the town grew in two parishes, Saint-Sulpice for the lower town and Saint-Léonard for the upper. Tanners, weavers and drapers developed their crafts in the lower town. A belfry, the first built in Brittany, was erected in 1397 with money from local merchants, who modelled it on Flemish examples seen in their travels. The castle formed part of the Duchy of Brittany's ultimately unsuccessful defence against French aggression, together with Vitré.</p><p>In the 16th century the town lost its defensive role. During the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolution">French Revolution</a> the Civil Constitution of the Clergy and the levée en masse triggered the rebellion known as the Chouannerie. In 1793 the Vendéens and Chouans seized the town, and Republicans took it a few weeks later. For eight years the town and its region passed from hand to hand, with massacres and looting, and the Chouan leader in the Fougères area was the young general Aimé du Boisguy. In the 19th century shoemaking replaced older crafts, and the population rose from 9,344 in 1856 to 20,952 in 1901. Workers struck in the shoe factories in the winter of 1906–07, and Jean Jaurès came to support them.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Fougères has been classified as a town of art and history since 1985. Its castle, a medieval stronghold on a granite ledge, is its most visited site. A large section of the town wall survives, and the Notre-Dame Gate of the 15th century, with a double drawbridge, is the only fortified town gate left. The churches of Saint-Sulpice and Saint-Léonard both date from the 15th century, and the town hall was completed in 1535. Shoemaking, once the town's chief industry, has almost disappeared. The Tour de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> has started and finished stages in the town.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Fougères” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The dukes guarded the march with Anjou and Poitou with fortresses such as Clisson, <b>Fougères</b> and Châteaubriant.</blockquote><a href="/empire/duchy-of-brittany">Duchy of Brittany · Military</a></li></ul></section>
